WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.890
Deep under northern England lies a
layer of rock called the Bowland Shale.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.300
The British Geological
Survey's central estimate:

3
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:13.630
1,329 trillion cubic
feet of gas in the rock.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.830
In 2000, Britain produced 112
per cent of the gas it used.

5
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.250
By 2024, only about 50 per cent.

6
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.890
Preston New Road, in Lancashire,
was fracked in 2018 and 2019.

7
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.650
Then fracking in England stopped.

8
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.290
So why did it stop?

9
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.670
Did the gas run out?

10
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.370
Was it too expensive?

11
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.610
In this mission, you'll see how fracking
cracks open shale to let the gas out.

12
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.690
Then you'll weigh the
case for and against,

13
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.930
using the evidence from Preston New Road.

14
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.600
Your mission: find out why fracking
for shale gas stopped in England.
